# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## Overview
HuLa is a modern, cross-platform Instant Messaging (IM) system. It leverages **Tauri v2** for the application container, **Vite 7** for fast frontend tooling, **Vue 3** for the user interface, and **TypeScript** for type safety. The backend logic is implemented in **Rust**.

The project supports:
- **Desktop:** Windows, macOS, Linux
- **Mobile:** Android, iOS

## Tech Stack

### Frontend
- **Framework:** Vue 3 (Composition API)
- **Language:** TypeScript
- **Build Tool:** Vite 7
- **State Management:** Pinia (with persistence plugins)
- **Routing:** Vue Router
- **Styling:** UnoCSS, Sass
- **UI Libraries:** Naive UI (Desktop), Vant (Mobile)
- **I18n:** vue-i18n

### Backend (Rust / Tauri)
- **Core:** Tauri v2
- **Database:** SQLite (managed via SeaORM with SQLCipher support)
- **Async Runtime:** Tokio
- **HTTP Client:** Reqwest
- **WebSocket:** tokio-tungstenite
- **Audio:** Rodio

### Matrix Protocol Integration
- **SDK:** Local `matrix-js-sdk` (linked via `link:../matrix-js-sdk`)
- **Backend:** [synapse-rust](https://github.com/langkebo/synapse-rust) - Rust-based Matrix Homeserver
- **API Contract:** All SDK calls follow the [matrix-js-sdk API contract](./matrix-js-sdk/docs/api-contract/)
- **Error Handling:** Uses `throwOnError` mode - read operations throw exceptions by default for error observability
- **Extensions:** Includes synapse-rust specific extensions (friend relationships, widgets, voice messages)

## Development Workflow

### Prerequisites
- Node.js (v20+ recommended)
- pnpm (v10+ recommended)
- Rust (latest stable)
- Android Studio / Xcode (for mobile development)

### Local Development Setup
1. Clone the repository and the sibling `matrix-js-sdk` repository
2. The project uses `link:../matrix-js-sdk` for local SDK integration
3. Run `pnpm install` to install dependencies and link the SDK
4. Ensure you have access to a synapse-rust backend instance (see [synapse-rust](https://github.com/langkebo/synapse-rust))
5. Configure backend URL in `.env.local` (never commit this file)

### Testing Strategy
- **Unit Tests:** Vitest for service layer and utilities (`src/**/__tests__/*.test.ts`)
- **E2E Tests:** Playwright for end-to-end flows (`e2e/*.spec.ts`)
- **Coverage:** Run `pnpm coverage` to generate coverage reports
- **Type Safety:** Always run `vue-tsc --noEmit` before committing

### Key Commands

| Action | Command | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Install Dependencies** | `pnpm install` | Installs Node.js dependencies. |
| **Start Desktop Dev** | `pnpm tauri:dev` or `pnpm td` | Starts the Tauri development server for desktop. |
| **Build Desktop** | `pnpm tauri:build` or `pnpm tb` | Builds the production application for desktop (interactive). |
| **Start Android Dev** | `pnpm adev` | Starts Android development (requires Android Studio). |
| **Start iOS Dev** | `pnpm idev` | Starts iOS development (requires Xcode, macOS only). |
| **Android Frontend Only (Windows)** | `pnpm adev:win` | Starts Android frontend without Tauri (Windows). |
| **iOS Frontend Only (macOS)** | `pnpm idev:mac` | Starts iOS frontend without Tauri (macOS). |
| **Commit Changes** | `pnpm commit` | Interactive git commit using Commitizen. |
| **Lint/Format** | `pnpm check` | Checks code using Biome (read-only). |
| **Fix Lint/Format** | `pnpm check:write` | Fixes code issues using Biome. |
| **Format Vue Templates** | `pnpm format:vue` | Formats Vue templates with Prettier. |
| **Format All** | `pnpm format:all` | Runs Biome + Prettier on all files. |
| **Run Tests** | `pnpm test:run` | Runs unit tests with Vitest. |
| **Test with UI** | `pnpm test:ui` | Opens Vitest UI with coverage. |
| **Test Coverage** | `pnpm coverage` | Generates test coverage report. |
| **E2E Tests** | `pnpm test:e2e` | Runs Playwright end-to-end tests. |
| **Type Check** | `vue-tsc --noEmit` | Runs TypeScript type checking. |
| **Link HuLa Skill** | `pnpm skill` | Links the custom HuLa skill for development. |

### Directory Structure

- **`src/`**: Frontend source code.
    - **`views/`**: Page components.
    - **`stores/`**: Pinia stores.
    - **`services/`**: API and service layers.
        - **`matrix/`**: Matrix protocol service wrappers (auth, rooms, messages, media, etc.).
        - **`backend/`**: Backend configuration and API clients.
        - **`offline/`**: Offline message handling and network services.
        - **`performance/`**: Performance optimization services (chunk uploads, etc.).
    - **`components/`**: Reusable Vue components.
    - **`layout/`**: App layout structures.
- **`src-tauri/`**: Rust backend source code.
    - **`src/command/`**: Tauri command handlers (exposed to frontend via `invoke`).
    - **`src/repository/`**: Database repository layer (SeaORM).
    - **`src/common/`**: Shared utilities and helpers.
    - **`entity/`**: SeaORM entity definitions.
    - **`migration/`**: Database migrations.
    - **`tauri.conf.json`**: Tauri configuration.
- **`tauri-plugin-hula/`**: Custom local Tauri plugin.
- **`matrix-js-sdk/`**: Local Matrix SDK (sibling directory, linked via pnpm).
- **`skills/hula-skill/`**: Custom development skill templates.

## Coding Style & Naming Conventions

- Indent 2 spaces, LF endings, trim whitespace (see `.editorconfig`).
- Format/lint with Biome: `pnpm check` (read-only) / `pnpm check:write` (fixes). Vue templates also use Prettier: `pnpm format:vue` or `pnpm format:all`.
- Prefer import aliases: `@/` → `src/`, `~/` → repo root.
- Naming: components `PascalCase.vue`, composables `useXxx.ts`, Pinia stores in `src/stores/`.
- **Commits:** Use `pnpm commit` to enforce Conventional Commits.
- **Styling:** Use UnoCSS utility classes where possible.
- **State:** Use Pinia for global state; prefer Composition API `<script setup>`.
- **Database:** Use SeaORM entities for database interactions.
- **Services:** Matrix operations must go through service classes in `src/services/matrix/`, never call SDK directly from components.
- **Tauri Commands:** Add new Rust commands in `src-tauri/src/command/` and register in `lib.rs`.

## Architecture Notes

### Three-Layer Architecture
HuLa follows a three-layer architecture:
1. **Frontend Application** (Tauri + Vue 3 + TypeScript): Cross-platform desktop/mobile UI
2. **SDK Layer** (matrix-js-sdk): Matrix protocol encapsulation and extensions
3. **Backend Service** (synapse-rust): Rust-based Matrix Homeserver

### Communication Patterns
- **Real-time Messaging:** WebSockets (`tokio-tungstenite` on Rust side, matrix-js-sdk on frontend)
- **Frontend ↔ Rust:** Tauri commands (via `invoke`) defined in `src-tauri/src/command/`
- **Frontend ↔ Backend:** Matrix Client API through local matrix-js-sdk
- **Service Layer:** All Matrix operations go through service classes in `src/services/matrix/`

### Key Architectural Patterns
- **Service Layer Pattern:** Matrix SDK is wrapped in service classes (e.g., `MatrixAuthService`, `MatrixRoomService`, `MatrixMessageService`)
- **Repository Pattern:** Database access is abstracted through repositories in `src-tauri/src/repository/`
- **Command Pattern:** Tauri commands expose Rust functionality to frontend
- **Manager Extensions:** matrix-js-sdk includes custom manager extensions for synapse-rust specific features

### Security
- **SQLCipher:** Encrypted local storage for sensitive data
- **Token Management:** Access tokens managed securely through Tauri backend
- **Error Handling:** throwOnError mode ensures all errors are caught and logged

### Platform-Specific Code
- Desktop-specific code: `#[cfg(desktop)]` in Rust, platform checks in TypeScript
- Mobile-specific code: `#[cfg(mobile)]` in Rust, `TAURI_ENV_PLATFORM` checks in frontend
- Vite config adjusts ports and components based on platform (desktop: 6130, mobile: 5210)

## Security & Configuration

- Don’t add secrets to tracked files. Use `.env.local` for personal tokens/keys.
- Package installs default to the registry in `.npmrc`; if it’s unavailable, override locally: `pnpm config set registry https://registry.npmjs.org/`.

## Important

- Do not prefix unused variables with an underscore, delete them instead
- Do not use emojis in commit messages, logs, or documentation
- Never change the .rules file unless the user specifically asks for it

## Pinia

This project uses Pinia for state management with specific patterns:

- Always create stores with the setup-style `defineStore('name', () => { ... })` for better type safety and composition.
- Use `storeToRefs` when destructuring state so reactivity is preserved.
- Group business logic inside the store's actions; components should only call actions/state.
- When a store depends on another store, import and call the other store factory inside the setup to share a single instance.
- Use `pinia-plugin-persistedstate` (already registered globally) for stores that must survive reloads—opt in per store via `persist: true`.

### Store Access Patterns

- Access other stores inside Pinia actions by instantiating the store at the top of the action: `const settings = useEditorSettingsStore();`
- Prefer reading dependent store state inside actions rather than passing parameters through components.
- Keep all imperative logic inside actions; components should remain declarative and simple.
- Avoid exporting raw refs outside of the store unless absolutely necessary; expose derived state through getters instead.

### CSS Variables & UnoCSS

Theme tokens live in `src/styles/scss/global/variable.scss`, but prefer inline UnoCSS utilities for simple light/dark styling.

**Defining Tokens**
- Default to per-element classes such as `bg-[lightColor] dark:bg-[darkColor]` or `text-[lightText] dark:text-[darkText]` so colors stay close to the component.
- Promote a color to `variable.scss` only when it is reused across multiple components or represents a semantic token (e.g. menu background).
- Keep light values on `:root` and dark overrides under `html[data-theme="dark"]` to leverage the existing data attribute toggle.
- When adding gradient or complex values, still store them as a variable (see `--bg-menu`) and document them inline in `variable.scss`.

**Using Tokens with UnoCSS**
- Prefer UnoCSS bracket syntax to consume tokens: `bg-[--center-bg-color]`, `text-[--text-color]`, `border-[--line-color]`.
- For multi-property helpers, apply directives are available because `@unocss/transformer-directives` is enabled: `@apply text-[--text-color]`.
- When a component needs conditional theming, toggle `data-theme` on `<html>` (light/dark) or add scoped data attributes (e.g. `data-theme="compact"`) and extend `variable.scss` with the selector.

### language
- The language of the reply is determined based on the language of the user's question. For example, if a user asks a question in simplified Chinese, reply in simplified Chinese.

## Working with Matrix SDK

### SDK Integration
- The project uses a **local** matrix-js-sdk located at `../matrix-js-sdk` (sibling directory)
- Linked via `"matrix-js-sdk": "link:../matrix-js-sdk"` in package.json
- Never modify SDK code directly; propose changes to the SDK repository instead

### Service Layer Pattern
- All Matrix SDK calls must go through service classes in `src/services/matrix/`
- Service classes wrap SDK functionality and handle error translation
- Example services: `MatrixAuthService`, `MatrixRoomService`, `MatrixMessageService`, `MatrixMediaService`
- Components should never import or call matrix-js-sdk directly

### Error Handling
- SDK uses `throwOnError` mode: read operations throw exceptions by default
- Always wrap SDK calls in try-catch blocks within service methods
- Log errors using `@tauri-apps/plugin-log` (info, error, warn functions)
- Translate SDK errors to user-friendly messages in the service layer

### API Contract
- Follow the [matrix-js-sdk API contract](./matrix-js-sdk/docs/api-contract/) for all SDK interactions
- Respect the contract's error handling patterns and return types
- When adding new SDK features, ensure they follow the established contract patterns






